@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Optimization — Selection Map (is there a gradient?)
' Synthesis: the fitting engine shared by every other family in this folder
' Level:  1

actor "Human expert\nobjective to minimize" as H

component "Gradient available\nSGD, momentum, Adam" as GRAD <<app>>
component "No gradient\nCMA-ES, hill-climbing" as BB <<host>>
database  "Parameter vector θ" as THETA <<db>>

H ARROW_MAIN GRAD : loss is differentiable\nand cheap to evaluate
H ARROW_MAIN BB : objective is a simulation,\na match result, or a ranking
GRAD ARROW_MAIN THETA : many small steps
BB ARROW_QUEUE THETA : few expensive evaluations

note bottom of THETA
  **The dividing question is a gradient, not scale.**
  A neural net exposes ∂Loss/∂θ, so it takes
  gradient steps (""gradient-descent.puml"").
  A hand-tuned weight vector scored by //playing
  games// exposes no derivative — the objective is a
  noisy black box, so it needs population search
  (""cma-es.puml"").
  ----
  This is the seam where a heuristic evaluator stops
  being argued about and starts being measured: make
  the weights a vector, define a fitness signal
  (""../rating-systems/""), then search.
end note
@enduml
